Identifying a system’s Lagrangian from trajectory observations is an ill-posed problem [ 21 ] , as the Lagrangian of a given system is non-unique. In other words, a single observation trajectory can be explained by different Lagrangians. Even more severe, the learned Lagrangian can be degenerate (i. e., irregular), for instance, when a null-Lagrangian is found. For discrete Lagrangians to be non-degenerate (i. e., regular), ∂ 2 L Δ ∂ 𝒒 b ​ ∂ 𝒒 a  rac{\partial^{2}L_{\Delta}}{\partial m{q}_{b}\partial m{q}_{a}} needs to be invertible everywhere [ 11 , 21 ] . In most literature, the ambiguity and regularity of learned Lagrangians are not considered or only implicitly enforced via specific assumptions, such as specific Lagrangian structures or availability of external force measurements [ 16 , 22 , 25 , 26 ] . Thus, in contrast to previous work on learning non-conservative dynamics, we introduce normalization conditions to ensure the non-degeneracy of the learned Lagrangians, following [ 21 ] . Details on the regularity and ambiguity of the learned Lagrangian are given in Appendix A.5 . Following the lines of [ 21 ] , we consider 2 ​ n q + 1 2n_{q}+1 normalization conditions. To this end, we enforce—at some anchor points 𝒓 ¯ L  ar{ m{r}}_{L} and 𝒔 ¯ F  ar{ m{s}}_{F} —a fixed value n L ≠ 0 n_{L}
