We test the lgp in a quantitative simulation study with controlled, damped multi-link pendulums. We randomly generate N ∈ { 25 , 50 , 100 , 200 , 300 } N\in\{25,50,100,200,300\} angle measurement triplets { 𝒒 n − 1 ( i ) , 𝒒 n ( i ) , 𝒒 n + 1 ( i ) } i = 1 N \{ m{q}_{n-1}^{(i)}, m{q}_{n}^{(i)}, m{q}_{n+1}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N} from single, double, and triple pendulums, i. e., n q ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 } n_{q}\in\{1,2,3\} , with varying torque inputs 𝒖  m{u} . Figure 2(a) illustrates how additional physics information in the kernel choice can improve the accuracy of the learned Lagrangian and force gp in terms of their posterior mean and covariance. We highlight that—without further assumptions—the Lagrangian and external force function of a system cannot be determined uniquely from trajectory data [ 21 ] . Instead, a single trajectory can be generated from different equivalent or alternative Lagrangians [ 21 ] . In this light, the lgp learn the true Lagrangian up to a scaling operation, under certain conditions detailed in Appendix A.5 . Regardless, the lgp provide a valid Euler-Lagrange operator, useful for prediction tasks. A quantitative study of such predictions with varying system dimensions n q n_{q} and varying amounts of training data N N shows that the lgp outperform the standard gp (see Figure 2(b) and Appendix C.1 ). Moreover, incorporating a physics-inspired kernel in the lgp improves the predictive performance and the generalization beyond the training domain further (see Appendix A.5 ). (a) Learned Lagrangian and force gp in a single pendulum, i. e., n q = 1 n_{q}=1 ( N = 300 N=300 data points, dashed contours: true L L and 𝑭  m{F} ). (b) Error against training data budget in 20 20 -step simulations ( n q = 1 n_{q}=1 , 50 50 random simulations). Figure 2 : The proposed lgp enable learning probabilistic models of a simulated pendulum’s Lagrangian L L and external forces 𝑭  m{F} only from position data. Incorporating further physics knowledge into the kernel improves the accuracy of L L and 𝑭  m{F} as well as the predictive performance. See Appendices A.5 and C for further details. By using variational discretization schemes in Section 3 , the learned lgp preserve the symplectic structure of the Lagrange-d’Alembert principle ( 1 ) by construction. In practice, this means that a set of initial conditions preserves its volume if predicted forward in time. In turn, this provides—in a conservative system, i. e., without external forces—an approximately constant energy level of the forward predictions. In fact, it oscillates around the initial energy, corresponding to constant-volume deformations of the set of initial conditions. We observe this property in Figure 3(a) and Appendix C.2 , where we validate in a conservative pendulum that the structure-preserving lgp yield accurate long-term predictions without energy drift, unlike the standard gp . As an aside, Figure 3(a) visualizes that we can compute any linear observable of the Lagrangian, such as the Hamiltonian, induced by the linear operator ( 3 ) (see details in Appendix A.3 ). In Figure 3(b) , we investigate the dependence on time step sizes. First, we train at different time step sizes Δ ​ t train \Delta t_{\mathrm{train}} and predict at time steps equal to those in the corresponding training data. Second, we show that the continous-time lgp generalizes to custom prediction steps, i. e., Δ ​ t train ≠ Δ ​ t pred \Delta t_{\mathrm{train}}
